gano NEWS

* 0.0.1 (unreleased)

First working version of the editor.

- A nano-style terminal editor: open, edit and save UTF-8 files (line
  endings and the final newline are kept); cut/paste, undo/redo, search,
  go to line, write out; prompts in the status line; redraws on resize.
- Character buffer (src/gano/cbuf-*.scm) with undo and redo for every
  editing command, recorded as partial line state per operation.
- Text is laid out left to right, with wide characters, tabs, combining
  and spacing marks, and control characters measured per Markus Kuhn's
  wcwidth (tables generated from Unicode 15.1).  Bidirectional reordering
  is left to the terminal.
- A file that is not valid UTF-8 opens with its bad bytes shown as
  U+FFFD, and saving over it asks first.  A file that cannot be read
  (a directory, say) is reported plainly.
- ^K cuts the selection if there is one, else the line, and A-6 copies
  the same way, as in nano; ^U pastes either.
- The terminal is brought up to date with as few bytes as possible
  (src/gano/screen.scm) rather than redrawn: the editor works out what
  turns the screen it last drew into the new one, using scrolling
  (regions too), inserting and deleting lines and characters, erasing,
  repeats and the shortest cursor moves.  Typing a character sends
  about 25 bytes where a redraw sent 2000, so the editor stays
  responsive on a slow line.  ^L (the redraw command) redraws
  everything, in any mode, for when the terminal has been garbled.
- gano --help, gano --version; "--" ends the options.
- Plain Makefile: make, make check, make dist.
- Command registry (src/gano/commands.scm): each command has a short
  name, a help line, the modes it works in with their shortcuts, and the
  inputs it asks for.  The standard table covers the buffer and frame
  operations.  The editor takes its keys from it in every mode, the
  prompts included (so ^Y/^V jump to the first/last line while typing a
  search), prompting for any inputs a command needs.
- A-x runs any command by name, with Tab completion.
- Help comes from the command table: the help rows show the current
  mode's shortcuts, and ^G (or F1) opens a help screen listing every
  command with its keys and help line.
- Interactive replace (^\ or A-r): Y/N at each match, A for all the
  rest, ^C to stop; it wraps round to where it began.  The match being
  asked about, and any selection, are shown in reverse video.
- Commands report what happened ("Not found", "Replaced 3
  occurrences").  Scrolling the view takes the cursor along, as in nano.
